 MS MARCO-XI (Hindi). It's QnA format — one query, 10 candidate passages,
 relevance labels — not a document corpus. Indexed subset: 2,144 chunks
 (bounded from the full 778K for local dev; documented, not hidden).
+
+## Deploying on Railway
+
+Railway does not run `docker compose`. The stack is split into one Railway
+**service per directory**, each with its own `Dockerfile` + `railway.json`:
+
+| Service      | Directory    | Notes                                                        |
+| ------------ | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
+| qdrant       | `qdrant/`    | `qdrant/qdrant:v1.10.1`. Mount a volume at `/qdrant/storage`. |
+| embedding    | `embedding/` | TEI `bge-m3`. Mount a volume at `/data` (model cache).       |
+| backend      | `backend/`   | Flask + gunicorn. Binds to Railway's `PORT`.                 |
+| frontend     | `frontend/`  | Next.js standalone, served by `bun server.js` on `PORT`.     |
+| reranker     | `reranker/`  | **Optional.** Only if you enable reranking.                  |
+
+### 1. Create the project + services
+
+Create a Railway project, then add a service from this repo for each
+directory above (New Service → GitHub Repo → set Root Directory to
+`qdrant/`, `embedding/`, `backend/`, `frontend/`, and optionally
+`reranker/`). Railway auto-detects the `Dockerfile` in each.
+
+### 2. Add persistent volumes
+
+- **qdrant**: add a volume mounted at `/qdrant/storage`.
+- **embedding**: add a volume mounted at `/data` (so the bge-m3 model
+  isn't re-downloaded on every redeploy — first start downloads ~2GB).
+
+### 3. Wire services together (private networking)
+
+Railway private DNS: `<service>.railway.internal` resolves to that
+service on its listening port. Use the **service name you created**.
+
+On the **backend** service, set:
+
+```
+QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant.railway.internal:6333
+EMBEDDING_SERVICE_URL=http://embedding.railway.internal:80
+# only if you deployed the reranker:
+RERANKER_SERVICE_URL=http://reranker.railway.internal:80
+```
+
+Plus the secret keys (see `backend/.env.example`):
+
+```
+FLASK_SECRET_KEY=<python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))">
+SARVAM_API_KEY=<...>
+OLLAMA_API_KEY=<...>
+CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://<your-frontend-domain>.up.railway.app
+```
+
+On the **frontend** service, set (this is inlined into the client bundle
+at build time, so it must be present before the first build):
+
+```
+NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://<your-backend-domain>.up.railway.app
+```
+
+### 4. Deploy order
+
+1. `qdrant` and `embedding` first (backend's `/health` waits on Qdrant;
+   embedding needs its first-boot model download — give it a few minutes).
+2. `backend` — confirm `/health` returns `status: ok`.
+3. `frontend`.
+
+### 5. Index the dataset (one-time)
+
+The Railway backend container has no indexed data yet. Run ingestion
+against the deployed Qdrant + embedding services from your machine:
+
+```bash
+cd backend
+# point at the Railway-deployed services, not localhost
+export QDRANT_URL=https://<your-qdrant-domain>.up.railway.app
+export QDRANT_API_KEY=            # set if you enabled Qdrant auth
+export EMBEDDING_SERVICE_URL=https://<your-embedding-domain>.up.railway.app
+export SARVAM_API_KEY=... OLLAMA_API_KEY=... FLASK_SECRET_KEY=...
+uv run python scripts/run_ingestion.py --limit 2000
+```
+
+(Or use `railway run` inside the backend service to execute the script
+in-service against the private network.)
+
+### Notes
+
+- The backend Dockerfile now binds to `${PORT}` (Railway-injected) with a
+  fallback to `8000` for local `docker build` runs.
+- The frontend uses Next.js `output: "standalone"` for a small runtime
+  image with no `node_modules`.
+- TEI on CPU is slow to boot (model load) — `embedding`'s healthcheck
+  timeout is set to 300s. Expect ~2–5 min first deploy.
+
